  "glossaryHelp": "Help: glossary",
  "glossaryOneRmAbbr": "1RM",
  "glossaryOneRmName": "One-repetition maximum",
  "glossaryOneRmWhat": "The heaviest weight you could lift for a single clean repetition of an exercise. It is usually estimated from a lighter set instead of tested directly.",
  "glossaryOneRmHow": "It gives you one number to compare your strength over time and to pick training weights as a percentage, e.g. 70-85% for strength work.",
  "glossaryOneRmExample": "You bench 80 kg for 5 reps. The Epley estimate is about 93 kg, so a set at 80% would use roughly 75 kg.",
  "glossaryRirAbbr": "RIR",
  "glossaryRirName": "Reps in reserve",
  "glossaryRirWhat": "How many more repetitions you could still do with good form when you stop a set. 0 RIR means you could not do another rep.",
  "glossaryRirHow": "It keeps sets hard enough to grow stronger without grinding to failure every time, and tells you when to add weight.",
  "glossaryRirExample": "You plan 8 reps at 2 RIR. If you finish 8 and feel you had 4 left, the weight is too light and you can increase it.",
  "glossaryRpeAbbr": "RPE",
  "glossaryRpeName": "Rate of perceived exertion",
  "glossaryRpeWhat": "A 1 to 10 score of how hard a set felt. RPE 10 is maximal effort, RPE 8 means about two reps were left.",
  "glossaryRpeHow": "It lets you autoregulate: on a bad day you lower the weight to hit the target RPE, on a good day you can do more.",
  "glossaryRpeExample": "Squats at RPE 8 felt like you had two clean reps left. Next week you try the same weight for RPE 7.5.",
  "glossaryDoubleProgressionAbbr": "Double progression",
  "glossaryDoubleProgressionName": "Double progression",
  "glossaryDoubleProgressionWhat": "A progression method with a rep range, for example 8-12. You keep the weight until you reach the top of the range on every set, then you add weight and start at the bottom again.",
  "glossaryDoubleProgressionHow": "It makes progress simple and measurable without needing a complicated plan, and works well for muscle growth.",
  "glossaryDoubleProgressionExample": "Dumbbell press 3 x 8-12 at 20 kg. Once you do 12, 12, 12 you move to 22 kg and aim for 8 reps again.",
  "glossaryDeloadAbbr": "Deload",
  "glossaryDeloadName": "Deload week",
  "glossaryDeloadWhat": "A planned lighter week with less weight or fewer sets so your body can recover from accumulated fatigue.",
  "glossaryDeloadHow": "It lowers the risk of injury and stalled progress, so you come back stronger in the following weeks.",
  "glossaryDeloadExample": "After four hard weeks you do your usual exercises with about 60% of the sets, or 10% less weight, for one week.",
  "glossarySupersetAbbr": "Superset",
  "glossarySupersetName": "Superset",
  "glossarySupersetWhat": "Two or more exercises done back to back with little or no rest in between, then a rest after the whole round.",
  "glossarySupersetHow": "It saves time and, when the exercises train opposite muscles, keeps performance high while the other muscle rests.",
  "glossarySupersetExample": "Bench press followed straight away by rows, then a 90 second rest, repeated for 3 rounds.",
  "glossaryAmrapAbbr": "AMRAP",
  "glossaryAmrapName": "As many reps as possible",
  "glossaryAmrapWhat": "A set where you continue until you cannot do another good repetition, or until the time is up.",
  "glossaryAmrapHow": "It shows your real capacity and is a useful test of progress, because more reps at the same weight means you got stronger.",
  "glossaryAmrapExample": "Push-ups AMRAP: you do 18 clean reps this week. Next month you aim for 20 or more.",
  "glossaryPrAbbr": "PR",
  "glossaryPrName": "Personal record",
  "glossaryPrWhat": "Your best result so far for an exercise: the heaviest weight, the most reps at a weight or the best estimated 1RM.",
  "glossaryPrHow": "PRs are a clear sign that your plan works and a good source of motivation.",
  "glossaryPrExample": "Last month you did 100 kg for 5 reps on deadlift. Today you did 100 kg for 6 reps, a new PR.",
  "glossaryVolumeAbbr": "Volume",
  "glossaryVolumeName": "Training volume",
  "glossaryVolumeWhat": "The total amount of work you do, usually counted as sets per muscle group per week, or as sets x reps x weight.",
  "glossaryVolumeHow": "Enough volume drives muscle growth, too much slows recovery. Tracking it shows whether you do enough for your goal.",
  "glossaryVolumeExample": "12 to 16 hard sets of chest per week is a common target. 4 sets of 10 reps at 50 kg is 2000 kg of volume.",
  "glossaryKcalAbbr": "kcal",
  "glossaryKcalName": "Kilocalories",
  "glossaryKcalWhat": "The unit for the energy in food and the energy you burn. One kcal is what people usually call a calorie.",
  "glossaryKcalHow": "Eating more than you burn leads to weight gain, less than you burn to weight loss. Your target kcal is how you steer this.",
  "glossaryKcalExample": "A maintenance intake of 2500 kcal and a goal to lose weight could mean eating about 2100 to 2250 kcal per day.",
  "glossaryMacrosAbbr": "Macros",
  "glossaryMacrosName": "Macronutrients",
  "glossaryMacrosWhat": "Protein, carbohydrates and fat: the three nutrients that provide energy and that you need in larger amounts. Protein and carbohydrates have 4 kcal per gram, fat has 9.",
  "glossaryMacrosHow": "The split affects recovery, energy and hunger. Enough protein protects muscle while you diet or build.",
  "glossaryMacrosExample": "A 80 kg lifter might eat 160 g protein, 250 g carbohydrates and 70 g fat, which is about 2270 kcal.",
  "glossaryNutriScoreAbbr": "Nutri-Score",
  "glossaryNutriScoreName": "Nutri-Score",
  "glossaryNutriScoreWhat": "A label from A (green) to E (red) that rates the overall nutritional quality of a food, based on things like sugar, salt, saturated fat, fibre and protein.",
  "glossaryNutriScoreHow": "It helps you pick the better option within the same type of food quickly. It does not replace your calorie and protein targets.",
  "glossaryNutriScoreExample": "Two breakfast cereals: one rated A and one rated D. The A one has less sugar and more fibre per 100 g.",
  "glossaryNovaAbbr": "NOVA",
  "glossaryNovaName": "NOVA food classification",
  "glossaryNovaWhat": "A scale from 1 to 4 that groups foods by how processed they are: 1 unprocessed, 2 culinary ingredients, 3 processed, 4 ultra-processed.",
  "glossaryNovaHow": "Diets high in group 4 foods tend to be easier to overeat. Choosing more group 1 and 2 foods helps with fullness and quality.",
  "glossaryNovaExample": "An apple is NOVA 1, olive oil is 2, canned beans are 3 and a sugary soft drink is 4.",
  "glossaryMovingAverage7Abbr": "7-day average",
  "glossaryMovingAverage7Name": "7-day moving average",
  "glossaryMovingAverage7What": "The average of the last seven days, recalculated every day. It smooths out the daily ups and downs.",
  "glossaryMovingAverage7How": "Body weight changes a lot from day to day because of water and food. The trend line shows whether you are really gaining or losing.",
  "glossaryMovingAverage7Example": "Your scale shows 81.2, 80.6, 81.5 and so on. The 7-day average of 81.0 is what you compare to last week.",
  "glossaryHrAbbr": "HR",
  "glossaryHrName": "Heart rate",
  "glossaryHrWhat": "How many times your heart beats per minute (bpm).",
  "glossaryHrHow": "It shows how hard you are working. Training in the right zone helps endurance and fat burning, and a lower resting HR over time shows fitness.",
  "glossaryHrExample": "A resting HR of 62 bpm and 150 bpm during intervals. A rough maximum is 220 minus your age.",
  "glossaryStepsPerMinAbbr": "steps/min",
  "glossaryStepsPerMinName": "Steps per minute (cadence)",
  "glossaryStepsPerMinWhat": "How many steps you take in one minute while walking or running.",
  "glossaryStepsPerMinHow": "Walking at around 100 steps per minute is moderate intensity, 130 or more is brisk. It is an easy way to check effort without a heart rate monitor.",
  "glossaryStepsPerMinExample": "A 30 minute walk at 110 steps per minute is about 3300 steps of moderate activity.",
  "glossaryBpmAbbr": "BPM",
  "glossaryBpmName": "Beats per minute",
  "glossaryBpmWhat": "The tempo of a song. Here it is used to pick music that matches the phase of your workout.",
  "glossaryBpmHow": "A steady beat helps you keep a rhythm and can make hard work feel easier. Slower music suits rest, faster music suits intervals.",
  "glossaryBpmExample": "100-120 BPM for warm-up, 120-140 for strength, 150-170 for HIIT and 90-110 for rest or cooldown.",
